{
  "_comment": "AI-DEV hub-owned Claude Code settings. Source of truth: <hub>/adapters/claude/settings.fragment.json. Two placeholders are substituted by `make sync`: __AI_DEV_HOME__ becomes $AI_DEV_HOME (wherever the hub is checked out) and __HOME__ becomes $HOME. A hub path must use __AI_DEV_HOME__ — spelled __HOME__/.ai-dev it would point at a directory that does not exist for anyone who cloned somewhere else, and a hook registered at a path that does not exist simply never runs. These keys are merged into ~/.claude/settings.json by `make -C ~/.ai-dev sync`; every other key in that file (theme, model, tui, ...) stays yours. Keys beginning with _ are notes and are stripped at merge. Do not hand-edit the merged copy.",

  "_note_excludedCommands": "Codex is excluded from Claude's sandbox deliberately. It authenticates against the OS keyring over the D-Bus session socket, and this sandbox blocks unix sockets by design — allowing that socket would hand every sandboxed command a path to gnome-keyring, which is exactly what the credential rules protect. Codex is not unprotected as a result: it enforces its own bubblewrap sandbox, default_permissions=':read-only' globally, and a stricter inline profile for the reviewer. Nesting bubblewrap inside bubblewrap would additionally require enableWeakerNestedSandbox, which is worse. Verified behaviourally by tests/codex-boundary.test.sh. The sync/manage entries are here because writing ~/.claude/settings.json is always denied inside the sandbox (protected settings file).",

  "_note_denyWrite": "~/.ai-dev is deliberately NOT in denyWrite. A deny beats the default cwd allow, so listing it would make the hub unwritable even from a session started inside it. The framework is protected from other projects by the sandbox's own cwd rule plus the framework-write rule in hooks/security-guard.sh.",

  "_note_denyWrite_breadth": "This list is broader than the credential set on purpose. AI-DEV does not assume any single sandbox boundary holds in every version and environment, so these entries are defence in depth rather than decoration: they cover the classes an agent would use to persist or to reach credentials — shell init files, autostart and systemd user units, PATH directories, and credential/VCS configuration. Treat the list as load-bearing. bin/doctor (sandbox:confinement) exercises it on every run and reports the outcome as its own category rather than folding it into a pass. Adding a path here is the fix for any newly identified persistence vector; re-check after every Claude Code upgrade.",

  "_note_strictAllowlist": "Without strictAllowlist, a host outside allowedDomains only *prompts* — and a non-interactive sandboxed command has nothing to prompt with, so an off-allowlist host stays reachable and only the explicit deniedDomains entries are refused. An exfiltration denylist that blocks only the endpoints someone thought to name is not a control. strictAllowlist denies everything outside the allowlist instead of prompting; it is honored only from user, managed and --settings scopes, so a repository cannot turn it off. Requires Claude Code 2.1.219+. It is read ONCE, when the session's proxy is built at session start, so a session that predates the `make sync` that wrote it keeps the old permissive proxy however current the file on disk is — restart Claude Code after a sync or the setting is not in effect. Because of that, bin/doctor (sandbox:network-live) defers instead of failing when it detects a stale session, and the authoritative behavioural check is bin/host-check --network, which builds a throwaway sandbox from the settings as they are now.",

  "_note_envScrub": "CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1 also isolates the PID namespace for Bash subprocesses on Linux, so ps/pgrep/kill inside a command cannot see host processes. Remove this key and re-sync if that ever blocks real work; sandbox.credentials.envVars still covers the credential variables.",
  "_note_claudeMdExcludes": "tests/fixtures/hostile-project holds a hostile CLAUDE.md, skill, agent, command and hook set as test input. It is stored inert — non-discovery filenames, @@RUNDIR@@ placeholders instead of real paths — and the live copy is materialized into a gitignored directory only while the test runs. This exclusion is the third layer: even if a future fixture is committed under a live name, it can never enter a session's context. Managed policy CLAUDE.md cannot be excluded this way, which is why the entry is scoped to that one subtree and nothing broader.",
  "claudeMdExcludes": [
    "__AI_DEV_HOME__/tests/fixtures/hostile-project/**",
    "__AI_DEV_HOME__/tests/fixtures/.run-hostile/**"
  ],
